{"builder":"Goal: make one pull request that adds a `--shout` flag to greet-cli and meets every acceptance line of issue https://github.com/o/greet-cli/issues/12:\n1. node bin/greet.js --shout <name> prints the greeting in upper case\n2. without --shout the output is unchanged\n3. docs/index.md documents --shout\n4. after merge, the published docs page at https://o.github.io/greet-cli/ lists --shout\n\nContext: `bin/greet.js` takes the first argument that does not start with `--` as the name and prints `greeting(name)` from `src/greet.js` (`Hello, <name>!`) followed by a newline. Right now it ignores any `--` flags. With no name it prints `usage: greet <name>` to stderr and exits with code 2. `docs/index.md` is the docs page. `.github/workflows/pages.yml` publishes the `docs/` directory to GitHub Pages on every push to main, so line 4 is met when docs/index.md (inside docs/) mentions `--shout` and the PR gets merged. Tests live in `test/greet.test.js` and run with `npm test` (node --test).\n\nSteps:\n1. In `bin/greet.js`, detect `--shout` anywhere in `process.argv.slice(2)`. When it is there, print the whole greeting line in upper case, so `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada` prints `HELLO, ADA!` and a newline. Keep picking the name as the first argument that does not start with `--`, so `--shout` is never taken as the name, and `node bin/greet.js Ada --shout` works too. You may add the upper-casing in `src/greet.js` (for example an option or a separate export) as long as `greeting('Ada')` still returns `Hello, Ada!`.\n2. Without `--shout`, keep the output byte-for-byte the same: `node bin/greet.js Ada` prints `Hello, Ada!\\n`, and running with no name still prints `usage: greet <name>` to stderr and exits with code 2. Consider changing the usage text to mention `--shout`, but only to stderr's usage line. Leaving it unchanged is safest.\n3. Update `docs/index.md` to document `--shout`. Show the usage `node bin/greet.js --shout <name>` and say it prints the greeting in upper case, with an example such as `HELLO, ADA!`. The literal text `--shout` must appear in the file. Keep the docs in `docs/` so the existing Pages workflow publishes them. Do not change `.github/workflows/pages.yml` unless it is broken.\n4. Add tests to `test/greet.test.js`: one where the CLI with `--shout Ada` outputs `HELLO, ADA!\\n`, and keep the existing test that the CLI without the flag outputs `Hello, Ada!\\n`. Run `npm test` and make sure it passes.\n5. Run these by hand and confirm the results: `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada` → `HELLO, ADA!`. `node bin/greet.js Ada` → `Hello, Ada!`. `grep -n -- '--shout' docs/index.md` → at least one match.\n6. Open the pull request. In its description, note that the published docs page (https://o.github.io/greet-cli/) will list `--shout` only after merge, when the pages workflow deploys `docs/`. That page cannot be checked before merge.","verifier":"Check the PR head for issue https://github.com/o/greet-cli/issues/12. Run every command from the repository root at the PR head. There is one check per expect line, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/greet.js --shout <name> prints the greeting in upper case\"\nRun: `node bin/greet.js --shout Ada` and `node bin/greet.js --shout 'Ada Lovelace'`.\nPASS: the first prints exactly `HELLO, ADA!` and the second prints exactly `HELLO, ADA LOVELACE!`. Each is the upper-case form of the normal greeting `Hello, <name>!`, and the exit code is 0.\nFAIL: either output is not the upper-cased greeting (for example it is still mixed case, `--shout` is printed as the name, or the greeting text is different), or the command errors.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"without --shout the output is unchanged\"\nRun: `node bin/greet.js Ada | od -c`, `node bin/greet.js 'Ada Lovelace'`, and `node bin/greet.js; echo rc=$?`. Also run `npm test`.\nPASS: the outputs match the behaviour before the change. `node bin/greet.js Ada` prints exactly `Hello, Ada!` followed by one newline (od shows `H e l l o ,   A d a ! \\n`). `'Ada Lovelace'` prints `Hello, Ada Lovelace!`. With no argument, stderr shows `usage: greet <name>` and the output ends with `rc=2`. `npm test` reports `# fail 0`.\nFAIL: any of these outputs or exit codes differs from the above, or `npm test` reports a failure.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"docs/index.md documents --shout\"\nRun: `grep -n -- '--shout' docs/index.md`, then read docs/index.md.\nPASS: grep finds at least one match, and the file describes what `--shout` does (prints the greeting in upper case).\nFAIL: grep finds nothing, or the file mentions `--shout` without saying what it does.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"after merge, the published docs page at https://o.github.io/greet-cli/ lists --shout\"\nRun: `curl -sS -L https://o.github.io/greet-cli/ | grep -n -- '--shout'`.\nPASS: the PR has been merged, and the published page fetched by curl contains `--shout` (grep prints at least one match).\nFAIL: the page does not contain `--shout`, or it cannot be fetched (for example HTTP 404). If the PR is not merged yet, this condition cannot be met. Report FAIL and say the cause is that the PR is not yet merged/published, not a defect in the diff.\n\nAdvisory (not part of any PASS/FAIL):\n- Confirm `.github/workflows/pages.yml` still publishes the `docs/` directory on push to main. If it does, the change in check 3 should reach the published page after merge.\n- Try `node bin/greet.js Ada --shout` (flag after the name). It should also print `HELLO, ADA!`.\n- Check that `test/greet.test.js` has a test for the `--shout` output."}